class RandomizedCollection {
    vector<int> a;
    unordered_map<int, unordered_set<int>> pos;   // only present values are keys
 public:
    bool insert(int v) {
        bool fresh = !pos.count(v);
        pos[v].insert((int)a.size()); a.push_back(v);
        return fresh;
    }
    bool remove(int v) {
        auto it = pos.find(v);
        if (it == pos.end()) return false;
        int i = *it->second.begin(); it->second.erase(i);
        int last = a.back(); a.pop_back();
        if (i != (int)a.size()) {
            a[i] = last;
            pos[last].erase((int)a.size());
            pos[last].insert(i);
        }
        if (pos[v].empty()) pos.erase(v);
        return true;
    }
    int getRandom() { return a[rand() % a.size()]; }
};
```
:::

:::

::: {.p-complexity}
**Scan for a copy:** $O(n)$ per `insert` and `remove`, $O(1)$ per `getRandom`. **A slot set per value:** $O(1)$ expected per operation, but $O(\text{distinct values ever touched})$ memory because empty sets are never freed. **Delete emptied sets:** $O(1)$ expected per operation ($O(1)$ amortised for the appends) and $O(n)$ space, one array slot plus one set entry per stored copy.
:::

::: {.p-edge}
- **`insert`'s return value** is `true` only for the first copy: a second `1` returns `false` even though the multiset grew.
- **The chosen copy is the tail**: `i == n-1`; then nothing moves and the tail value's slot set must not get `i` back. The guard `i != len(a)` (after the pop) handles it.
- **The tail holds the same value as the victim** (`last == v`, another copy): the code discards `n-1` from `pos[v]` and adds `i`, which is correct because `pos[last]` *is* `pos[v]`. Check the emptiness of `pos[v]` only after this repair.
- **Removing an absent value** returns `false` and must not create a key.
:::

::: {.callout-warning title="Common mistakes"}
- **Forgetting to discard the tail's old slot.** When the tail moves from `n-1` to `i`, discard `n-1` from `pos[last]` *and* add `i`; skipping the discard leaves an index pointing past the end.
- **Insisting on a particular copy** (the first index, the oldest): the contract says *any* copy, and that freedom is what makes `remove` $O(1)$.
- **Using `if not self.pos[v]` on a `defaultdict`** as a presence test: it inserts an empty set for every value you ask about, which is the leak form 3 removes.
:::

**Variants.** @sec-ds-p3-freqstack also stores many copies of a value, but chooses among them by a secondary key (recency), so the value $\to$ slots map becomes a frequency $\to$ stack map.

::: {.practice}
- [LC 519 · Random Flip Matrix](https://leetcode.com/problems/random-flip-matrix/){difficulty="Medium"} — draw from a shrinking range of free cells without storing the range: a dictionary remaps a drawn index to the current tail, which is swap-with-last on a virtual array.
:::


## Pattern 2: Snapshots and versions — tag the write, copy nothing {#sec-ds-p2}

**From deleting the present to keeping the past.** Swap-with-last lets the current state change cheaply but forgets every earlier state. P2 adds one idea: tag each write with the version it belongs to, so that old states can be read (or returned to) without ever copying the array.

**Recognise it.** "Take a snapshot", "value at time $t$", "undo", "back and forward"; many cheap writes between expensive-looking copies.

::: {.callout-important title="Template"}
An $O(1)$ snapshot is a counter, never a copy:

- `snap()` returns `version` and then increments it; nothing is written.
- `set(i, v)` appends `(version, v)` to cell $i$'s history, or overwrites the last record if it already carries the current version.
- `get(i, s)` is the floor search `bisect_right(versions_i, s) - 1` (@sec-ds-a2, @sec-bst-a2); seed each cell with `(0, default)` or treat "no record" as the default.
- To go *back* rather than read old versions, keep one array and a cursor, or a log of old values to roll back.
:::

**Complexity.** $O(1)$ `snap` and `set`, $O(\log k)$ `get` for $k$ records on the cell, $O(n + \text{writes})$ space.

### Problem: Snapshot Array {#sec-ds-p2-snapshot}

::: {.p-statement}
Design a `SnapshotArray(length)` whose cells all start at $0$, with three calls:

- `set(index, val)` — overwrite `arr[index]` with `val`;
- `snap()` — take a snapshot and return its id, which is the number of earlier `snap()` calls ($0, 1, 2, \ldots$);
- `get(index, snap_id)` — the value `arr[index]` held **when snapshot `snap_id` was taken**.

$1 \le$ `length` $\le 5\cdot10^4$, $0 \le$ `val` $\le 10^9$, `snap_id` always names a snapshot already taken, and there are at most $5\cdot10^4$ calls in total. The LeetCode sample runs `SnapshotArray(3); set(0,5); snap() -> 0; set(0,6); get(0,0) -> 5`: cell $0$ reads $5$ under snapshot $0$ even though it now holds $6$.

Source: [LC 1146 · Snapshot Array](https://leetcode.com/problems/snapshot-array/){.solved difficulty="Medium"}
:::

::: {.p-intuition}
Copying the array on every `snap()` costs $O(n)$ per snapshot, yet between two snapshots only the cells that were `set` change. So `snap()` only advances a counter, and each cell keeps its own history of `(snap_id, value)` records, written only when the cell is set (@sec-ds-a2). `get(i, s)` asks cell `i` for its newest record with id $\le s$; ids on one cell only increase, so that is a binary search (@sec-bst-a2).
:::

::: {.p-approach}
1. **Copy the whole array.** `snap()` stores a copy; `get` indexes it. $O(n)$ per snapshot and $O(n \cdot \text{snaps})$ memory. This class is the naive model.
2. **Versioned columns.** `hist[i]` is a list of `(snap_id, value)`; `snap` bumps a counter, `set` appends under the current id (or overwrites the record already carrying it), `get` walks the column for the newest record with id $\le s$. $O(1)$ `snap`, $O(\text{writes to } i)$ `get`.
3. **Bisect the column.** The ids of one column are sorted, so `get` is `bisect_right(ids, s) - 1`, $O(\log k)$.

**Operation contract (final form).** Invariant **I**: `ids[i]` is strictly increasing, starts with the seed id $0$, and its last entry is at most the current `snap_id`; `vals[i][j]` is the value cell `i` held at the end of the period with id `ids[i][j]`, as far as writes so far tell.

| Operation | What it does | How **I** survives | Cost |
|---|---|---|---|
| `set(i, v)` | overwrite the last record if it carries the current id, else append `(snap_id, v)` | the new id is $\ge$ the last one; equal ids are merged, so the list stays strictly increasing | $O(1)$ amortised |
| `snap()` | `snap_id += 1`, return the old value | records are untouched; future writes get the larger id | $O(1)$ |
| `get(i, s)` | `p = bisect_right(ids[i], s) - 1`, return `vals[i][p]` | read only; $p \ge 0$ because of the seed record | $O(\log k_i)$ for $k_i$ records |
:::

The buttons are in order. Each one keeps the previous solution and changes only what that form changes.

