:::

::: {.p-complexity}
**Copy the whole array:** $O(1)$ `set` and `get`, $O(n)$ `snap`, $O(n \cdot \text{snaps})$ memory; at the limits ($5\cdot10^4$ cells and up to $5\cdot10^4$ snapshots) that is up to $2.5\cdot10^9$ stored integers, so it is not a usable answer. **Versioned columns:** $O(1)$ `set` and `snap`, $O(k_i)$ `get` for $k_i$ records on the column, $O(n + w)$ memory for $w$ writes. **Bisect the column:** the same, with $O(\log k_i)$ `get`.
:::

::: {.p-edge}
- **Two writes under the same snapshot id**: `set` must *overwrite* the record carrying the current id, or a column would hold two records with one id and `get` would depend on which one the search lands on.
- **`get` on a never-written cell**: the seed record `(0, 0)` makes id $0$ always a valid floor, so the answer is $0$ without a branch.
- **A cell written only after the queried snapshot**: `bisect_right(ids, s) - 1` stops at the newest record with id $\le s$, skipping the later write.
:::

::: {.callout-warning title="Common mistakes"}
- **`snap()` returning the bumped counter.** The id handed out must be the one the writes *so far* were tagged with: `return snap_id++` (C++) or bump-then-return-minus-one (Python).
- **`bisect_left` instead of `bisect_right`.** With ids $[0, 2, 5]$ and query $4$, the floor is index $1$; `bisect_left(ids, 4) - 1` happens to agree, but for a query equal to an id, say $2$, `bisect_left` gives index $1 - 1 = 0$, the record *before* the snapshot.
- **Appending unconditionally in `set`**: several writes under one id must collapse into the last one.
:::

**Variants.** [LC 981 · Time Based Key-Value Store](https://leetcode.com/problems/time-based-key-value-store/){difficulty="Medium"} is the same per-key history with timestamps chosen by the caller instead of a counter (@sec-lang-p4-timemap solves it). Full persistence, where an old version can itself be modified, needs a persistent tree and belongs to the tree volume.

::: {.practice}
- [LC 2034 · Stock Price Fluctuation](https://leetcode.com/problems/stock-price-fluctuation/){difficulty="Medium"} — records keyed by timestamp that may be corrected later: a per-key latest value plus an ordered structure for the extremes.
:::


[**From reading old versions to moving between them.** A snapshot array answers "what did cell $i$ hold at version $s$?" for any $s$. A browser history only ever moves a cursor back and forth along one line of versions, and a new visit throws away the versions ahead of the cursor, so one array and one index replace the per-cell histories.]{.flow-bridge}

### Problem: Design Browser History {#sec-ds-p2-history}

::: {.p-statement}
Simulate one browser tab. `BrowserHistory(homepage)` starts on `homepage`; then:

- `visit(url)` — go to `url` from the current page, **erasing all forward history**;
- `back(steps)` — move back up to `steps` pages (stop at the first page) and return the page you land on;
- `forward(steps)` — move forward up to `steps` pages (stop at the newest page) and return the page you land on.

URLs have at most $20$ characters, $1 \le$ `steps` $\le 100$, and there are at most $5000$ calls. The sample visits `google, facebook, youtube` from `leetcode`, then `back(1) = facebook`, `back(1) = google`, `forward(1) = facebook`, `visit(linkedin)`, `forward(2) = linkedin`, `back(2) = google`, `back(7) = leetcode`.

Source: [LC 1472 · Design Browser History](https://leetcode.com/problems/design-browser-history/){.solved difficulty="Medium"}
:::

::: {.p-intuition}
The history is a list of versions of "where the tab is", and a cursor says which version is current. `back` and `forward` only move the cursor, clamped to the list. `visit` discards every version after the cursor and appends the new page. The discarded suffix need not even be deleted: an `end` marker that says where the live history stops makes `visit` $O(1)$ and leaves stale pages beyond `end`, unreachable.
:::

::: {.p-approach}
1. **One list, one cursor.** `visit` deletes `hist[i+1:]` and appends; `back`/`forward` clamp the cursor. $O(1)$ navigation; `visit` costs $O(\text{dropped} + 1)$, which is $O(1)$ amortised because each page is dropped at most once. This class is the model for the stress tests.
2. **Two stacks.** `left` holds the past and the present (top = current page), `right` holds the forward pages. `back` moves pages from `left` to `right` one at a time, `forward` moves them back, `visit` clears `right`. $O(\text{steps})$ per move.
3. **Array with an end marker.** Keep the cursor and an `end` index one past the newest live page. `visit` writes at `cur + 1` (appending only when the array is full) and sets `end = cur + 2`; nothing is ever deleted. Every operation is $O(1)$ amortised.

**Operation contract (final form).** Invariant **I**: `arr[0..end-1]` is the live history, oldest first, and $0 \le$ `cur` $<$ `end` $\le$ `len(arr)`. Entries at index $\ge$ `end` are garbage.

| Operation | What it does | How **I** survives | Cost |
|---|---|---|---|
| `visit(url)` | `cur += 1`; write `url` at `cur` (append if `cur == len(arr)`); `end = cur + 1` | the live history is the old prefix up to the cursor plus the new page | $O(1)$ amortised |
| `back(k)` | `cur = max(0, cur - k)` | the clamp keeps `cur` $\ge 0$ | $O(1)$ |
| `forward(k)` | `cur = min(end - 1, cur + k)` | the clamp keeps `cur` $<$ `end` | $O(1)$ |
:::

The buttons are in order. Each one keeps the previous solution and changes only what that form changes.

