:::

::: {.p-complexity}
**Scan the counts:** $O(1)$ `inc`/`dec`, $O(k)$ per `get` for $k$ distinct keys. **Count buckets:** $O(1)$ `inc`/`dec`, $O(L)$ per `get` for $L$ distinct counts. **Linked buckets:** $O(1)$ expected for all four operations (one hash lookup for `at[k]` plus a constant number of pointer writes and set updates). Space is $O(k)$ for every form; the linked form has at most one node per distinct count, never an empty one between operations.
:::

::: {.p-edge}
- **`get*` on an empty store** returns `""`; the sentinel check `head.next is tail` catches it without touching any key set.
- **A bucket emptied by a move must be unlinked** (`if not node.keys: _unlink(node)`), otherwise a stale node corrupts the next `getMin`/`getMax`.
- **`dec` to zero** deletes the key entirely (`del self.at[key]`), so a later `inc` treats it as brand-new — a stale count $0$ node would sit between `head` and the count-$1$ bucket.
:::

::: {.callout-warning title="Common mistakes"}
- **Sorting on every `get`** — the linked list keeps counts ordered *by construction*; only adjacent nodes are ever touched because counts move by $\pm1$.
- **Reusing a non-adjacent node**: on `inc`, `node.next` must be exactly `count+1`; if not, you must create it (`_after`), else two levels share a node.
- **A new key** must go to a count-$1$ node right after `head`: reuse `head.next` if its count is $1$, otherwise splice a new node in. Appending it anywhere else breaks the ordering.
:::

**Variants.** This is @sec-ds-p3-freqstack read as a query rather than a pop: the count-indexed groups are the same idea, plus the linked ordering. The doubly linked backbone with a `key -> node` map is also the LRU cache (@sec-lang-p1-lru), where the order is recency instead of count.

::: {.practice}
- [LC 2349 · Design a Number Container System](https://leetcode.com/problems/design-a-number-container-system/){difficulty="Medium"} — `index -> number` plus `number -> ordered set of indices`, with a moved index leaving one group and joining another: the same two-way bookkeeping, with an ordered set where AllOne could use adjacency.
:::


## Pattern 4: Array-backed hashing — turn a key into an index {#sec-ds-p4}

**From counts to arbitrary keys.** P3 assumed that a hash map turns any key into a place to store its count. P4 builds that map on a plain array: first with keys small enough to be indices, then with a modulus and chains, then with probing inside the array itself.

**Recognise it.** "Design a hash map / hash set without libraries"; keys from a small range (rooms, letters, values up to $10^6$); an adversary who knows your hash.

::: {.callout-important title="Template"}
- **Direct address** (small universe $U$): `table[key]`, or one bit per key. $O(1)$ worst case, $O(U)$ memory.
- **Chaining**: bucket `h(k)` holds a list; cost $O(1 + \alpha)$ expected for $\alpha = n/m$. Resize (double $m$, **re-insert every key**) when $\alpha > 0.75$.
- **Open addressing**: probe `h(k), h(k)+1, ...` inside the array; delete with a **tombstone**; rebuild when live keys plus tombstones exceed half the table (@sec-ds-a4).
:::

**Complexity.** $O(1)$ expected per operation and $O(1)$ amortised for the resizes; $O(n)$ space (direct addressing: $O(U)$).

### Problem: Hotelier {#sec-ds-p4-hotel}

::: {.p-statement}
A hotel has $10$ rooms numbered $0\ldots 9$ from left to right, with an entrance at each end. You get $n \le 10^5$ events, processed in order:

- `L` — a guest arrives through the **left** entrance and takes the free room closest to it;
- `R` — a guest arrives through the **right** entrance and takes the free room closest to it;
- a digit `d` — the guest in room `d` leaves (the room is guaranteed to be occupied).

Whenever a guest arrives, at least one room is free. Print the occupancy as $10$ characters (`1` = occupied) after all events. Sample 1: `LLRL1RL1` $\to$ `1010000011`. Sample 2: `L0L0LLRR9` $\to$ `1100000010`.

Source: [CF 1200A · Hotelier](https://codeforces.com/problemset/problem/1200/A){.solved rating="800"}
:::

::: {.p-intuition}
The state is an array `occ[10]` indexed by the room number itself: a direct-address table (@sec-ds-a4), with no hashing because the key already is the slot. With ten rooms, scanning for the first free room is cheap and is the intended solution. The design lesson is how to avoid the scan when the array is large: keep two bounds, `pl` with every room left of it occupied and `pr` with every room right of it occupied, and walk a bound only when a guest arrives at its door.
:::

::: {.p-approach}
1. **Scan for a free room.** On `L` take the first `0` in `occ`, on `R` the last, on a digit clear the cell. $O(10)$ per event, $O(10n)$ in total: fast enough here, and the model for the stress test.
2. **Free-end bounds.** Keep `pl` and `pr`. On `L`, walk `pl` right past occupied rooms, then occupy `occ[pl]`; on `R`, mirror with `pr`; on a digit `d`, free the room and widen the bounds with `pl = min(pl, d)`, `pr = max(pr, d)`.

**Operation contract (final form).** Invariant **I**: every room with index $<$ `pl` is occupied, and every room with index $>$ `pr` is occupied.

| Event | What it does | How **I** survives | Cost |
|---|---|---|---|
| `L` | walk `pl` right while `occ[pl] == 1`, then set `occ[pl] = 1` | the walk only passes occupied rooms; a free room exists and lies in $[\,$`pl`$,$ `pr`$\,]$ by **I**, so the walk stops at the leftmost free room | $O(\text{rooms passed})$, at most $O(m)$ |
| `R` | mirror image with `pr` | same | $O(\text{rooms passed})$, at most $O(m)$ |
| digit `d` | `occ[d] = 0`; `pl = min(pl, d)`; `pr = max(pr, d)` | the only new free room is `d`, and both bounds now lie on its correct side | $O(1)$ |
:::

The buttons are in order. Each one keeps the previous solution and changes only what that form changes.

