:::

::: {.p-complexity}
**Direct addressing:** $O(1)$ worst case per operation, $O(U)$ space. **Chained buckets:** $O(1 + n/m)$ expected per operation for a fixed $m$, $O(n)$ worst case if every key collides; $O(m + n)$ space. **Grow and rehash:** $O(1)$ expected per operation, amortised over rehashes (a $\Theta(m + n)$ rehash happens only after $\Omega(m)$ inserts since the previous one); space $O(n)$, because $m = \Theta(n)$ as long as the map has not shrunk. "Expected" assumes the keys spread evenly; $k \bmod m$ on adversarial keys does not (@sec-hash-anti).
:::

::: {.p-edge}
- **Update vs insert in `put`**: scan the chain for an existing key and overwrite, else append — treating `put` as always-append duplicates the key and breaks `get`.
- **Re-homing on resize**: after doubling $m$ you cannot reuse old bucket positions; every pair's index $k \bmod m$ changes, so re-insert from scratch (the `_rehash` body).
- **Absence vs a stored $-1$**: because values are $\ge 0$, returning $-1$ is unambiguous; a map allowing negative values needs a separate "present" flag.
- **Removing never shrinks** this table: after many deletions $m$ can be much larger than $n$. Halving $m$ when $n < m/8$ (not $m/2$, which would make a put/remove pair at the boundary rehash every time) restores $O(n)$ space.
:::

::: {.callout-warning title="Common mistakes"}
- **Forgetting to re-hash on resize** and keeping pairs in their old buckets — the map silently loses entries the moment $m$ changes.
- **A power-of-two $m$ with `k & (m - 1)`** keeps only the low bits of the key, so keys that differ only in high bits (multiples of $1024$, say) all collide; mix the key first, or use a prime $m$ (@sec-hash-sets).
- **A fixed $m$ and no resize**, then claiming $O(1)$: the chains grow with $n$, so the true cost is $O(1 + n/m)$.
:::

**Variants.** The direct table is @sec-ds-p4-hotel's `occ[10]` with a bigger universe; both are "index equals key, no hashing". The rehash is the vector doubling of @sec-arr-vec applied to a table instead of a tail. @sec-ds-p4-hashset stores the same keys without chains, in the table's own cells.

::: {.practice}
- [CF 4C · Registration system](https://codeforces.com/problemset/problem/4/C){rating="1300"} — a name-to-count map with a lookup per request; write it once with your own chained table to see it work on strings.
:::


[**From chains to probing.** Chaining stores a list per bucket. A hash set can keep the keys in the array's own cells instead: collisions walk to the next cell, and a deletion must leave a tombstone so that the walks of other keys stay intact. When the universe is small enough, a bit array beats both.]{.flow-bridge}

### Problem: Design HashSet {#sec-ds-p4-hashset}

::: {.p-statement}
Build a hash set without any built-in hash table:

- `add(key)` — insert `key`;
- `contains(key)` — return whether `key` is in the set;
- `remove(key)` — delete `key`; if it is absent, do nothing.

$0 \le$ `key` $\le 10^6$ and there are at most $10^4$ calls. The sample runs `add(1), add(2), contains(1) = true, contains(3) = false, add(2), contains(2) = true, remove(2), contains(2) = false`.

Source: [LC 705 · Design HashSet](https://leetcode.com/problems/design-hashset/){.solved difficulty="Easy"}
:::

::: {.p-intuition}
A set only needs one bit per possible key, so with keys up to $10^6$ a **bit array** of $10^6 + 1$ bits (125 KB) is a complete answer. When the universe is too large for that, store the keys themselves in the table's cells: **open addressing** probes `h(k), h(k)+1, h(k)+2, …` until it finds the key or an empty cell. A deleted key must leave a **tombstone**, not an empty cell, or a later search would stop early and miss keys that were placed beyond it (@sec-ds-a4).
:::

::: {.p-approach}
1. **Scan a list.** Keep the keys in a list; every call scans it. $O(n)$ per call. This class is the naive model.
2. **Bit array.** One bit per key in the universe, packed eight to a byte; `add`, `remove` and `contains` set, clear and test one bit. $O(1)$ worst case, $O(U/8)$ bytes.
3. **Open addressing.** A table of `cap` cells (a power of two), each `EMPTY`, `TOMB` or a key. A key's search starts at a hashed position and walks right, wrapping around. Rebuild the table when live keys plus tombstones fill half of it. $O(1)$ expected per call, $O(n)$ memory.

**Operation contract (final form).** Invariant **I**: for every stored key $k$, no cell on its probe path, from `h(k)` up to the cell holding $k$, is `EMPTY`; and `used` (live keys plus tombstones) is at most `cap / 2`.

| Operation | What it does | How **I** survives | Cost |
|---|---|---|---|
| `add(k)` | probe from `h(k)`; stop if $k$ is found; otherwise write $k$ into the first tombstone seen, or the `EMPTY` cell that ended the probe; rebuild if `used` exceeds `cap / 2` | the new key's path consists of cells that were already non-empty | $O(1)$ expected, rebuild amortised |
| `contains(k)` | probe until $k$ or `EMPTY` | read only; by **I**, reaching `EMPTY` proves absence | $O(1)$ expected |
| `remove(k)` | probe; if found, overwrite with `TOMB` | the cell stays non-empty, so every other key's path is intact | $O(1)$ expected |
:::

The buttons are in order. Each one keeps the previous solution and changes only what that form changes.

::: {.panel-tabset .solution-forms}

