##### C++

```cpp
class MyHashSet {
    static constexpr long long EMPTY = -1, TOMB = -2;   // keys are >= 0
    vector<long long> slots;
    int cap, bits, live = 0, used = 0;
    int home(long long k) const {
        if (bits == 0) return 0;
        unsigned long long x = (unsigned long long)k * 0x9E3779B97F4A7C15ULL;
        return (int)(x >> (64 - bits));
    }
    // returns the cell holding k, or -1; *freeCell = first reusable cell on the path
    int findCell(long long k, int* freeCell) const {
        int i = home(k), fr = -1;
        while (true) {
            if (slots[i] == k) { *freeCell = fr; return i; }
            if (slots[i] == EMPTY) { *freeCell = fr < 0 ? i : fr; return -1; }
            if (slots[i] == TOMB && fr < 0) fr = i;
            i = (i + 1) & (cap - 1);
        }
    }
    void rebuild(int newCap) {
        vector<long long> old;
        for (long long s : slots) if (s >= 0) old.push_back(s);
        cap = newCap; bits = 0; while ((1 << bits) < cap) ++bits;
        slots.assign(cap, EMPTY); live = used = 0;
        for (long long k : old) add((int)k);
    }
 public:
    MyHashSet() : slots(8, EMPTY), cap(8), bits(3) {}
    void add(int key) {
        int fr, hit = findCell(key, &fr);
        if (hit >= 0) return;
        if (slots[fr] == EMPTY) ++used;
        slots[fr] = key; ++live;
        if (2 * used > cap) rebuild(4 * live > cap ? 2 * cap : cap);
    }
    void remove(int key) {
        int fr, hit = findCell(key, &fr);
        if (hit >= 0) { slots[hit] = TOMB; --live; }
    }
    bool contains(int key) { int fr; return findCell(key, &fr) >= 0; }
};
```
:::

:::

::: {.p-complexity}
**Scan a list:** $O(n)$ per call, $O(n)$ space. **Bit array:** $O(1)$ worst case per call, $O(U)$ bits of space ($U = 10^6 + 1$, 125 KB) allocated up front. **Open addressing:** $O(1)$ expected per call while the table is at most half used (the expected probe count of linear probing depends only on the fill ratio), plus an $O(\text{cap})$ rebuild that happens only after $\Omega(\text{cap})$ insertions, so $O(1)$ amortised; $O(n)$ space.
:::

::: {.p-edge}
- **Removing an absent key** does nothing; removing a key twice leaves one tombstone.
- **Re-adding a removed key** reuses the first tombstone on its path, so a remove/add cycle does not grow `used`.
- **A table full of tombstones**: without the rebuild, `contains` of an absent key would walk forever. Counting tombstones in `used` guarantees an `EMPTY` cell always exists.
:::

::: {.callout-warning title="Common mistakes"}
- **Deleting by writing `EMPTY`.** Insert $a$ and $b$ with the same home: $b$ lands one cell to the right. Remove $a$ by emptying its cell, and `contains(b)` stops at that empty cell and answers `false`.
- **Counting only live keys toward the load.** Tombstones lengthen probes just like keys; the resize test must use live keys plus tombstones.
- **`k % cap` with a power-of-two `cap`.** That keeps only the low bits of $k$; keys that are multiples of `cap` all start at cell $0$. Mix the key (here, a multiply and the top bits) before reducing it.
:::

**Variants.** Python's `dict` and `set` use open addressing (with a perturbed probe sequence rather than a linear one), and many fast C++ hash tables do too, because walking adjacent cells is cache-friendly (@sec-arr-cache). `std::vector<bool>` and `std::bitset` are the library forms of the bit array.

::: {.practice}
- [LC 217 · Contains Duplicate](https://leetcode.com/problems/contains-duplicate/){difficulty="Easy"} — answer it with your own open-addressing set instead of the built-in one, and test it on keys that are all multiples of a power of two.
:::


## Pattern 5: Lazy resets with timestamps — defer the clear {#sec-ds-p5}

**From storing keys to forgetting them cheaply.** P1–P4 make each single-cell operation $O(1)$. An operation on *all* cells (clear, assign, flip) still costs $O(n)$. P5 adds the last idea: record the global operation once (a clock, a base value, a flag) and let each cell catch up the next time it is touched. The problems climb from the plain timestamp, to a two-valued flag, to timestamps that answer a question by themselves, to a fold that never stores the items at all.

**Recognise it.** "Assign to every element", "reset", "flip all", many test cases on one big array; answers that depend only on *when* something first happened; aggregates (sum, count, average) over a growing set.

::: {.callout-important title="Template"}
Never do work you can defer with a tag:

- Keep a value beside a **stamp**; a global reset bumps the clock, a stale stamp means "reset" (@sec-ds-a5). For "assign all to $x$", a stale cell reads the stored `base`; refresh it before modifying it.
- For an involution such as "flip all", store each cell relative to a global flag and keep counters consistent (`ones = n - ones`).
- When only *first* touches matter, record first-arrival times and answer from them, without simulating.
- For an aggregate query, fold each new item into `(sum, count)` when it arrives, and pop pending records when they are used.
:::

**Complexity.** $O(1)$ per global operation and per touched cell; $O(n)$ space for the stamps (or none, with a sparse set).

### Problem: All Assign Point Add {#sec-ds-p5-assign}

::: {.p-statement}
You are given an array $A_1, \ldots, A_N$ and $Q$ queries, processed in order:

- `1 x` — assign $x$ to **every** element of $A$;
- `2 i x` — add $x$ to $A_i$;
- `3 i` — print $A_i$.

$1 \le N, Q \le 2\cdot10^5$, $0 \le A_i, x \le 10^9$, and at least one query is of type 3. Values can exceed $2^{31}$ (sample 2 adds $10^9$ seven times to $10^9$ and prints $8\cdot10^9$). Sample 1: $A = (3, 1, 4, 1, 5)$ with queries `3 2`, `2 3 4`, `3 3`, `1 1`, `2 3 4`, `3 3` prints `1, 8, 5`.

Source: [AC abc278_d · All Assign Point Add](https://atcoder.jp/contests/abc278/tasks/abc278_d){.solved}
:::

::: {.p-intuition}
An "assign everything" query is a reset to a value, and doing it cell by cell is $O(N)$, so $Q$ of them cost $O(NQ)$. Do it lazily instead (@sec-ds-a5): remember the assigned value and a clock that counts assignments. Each cell keeps the clock reading at its last write; a cell whose stamp is older than the clock is logically equal to the last assigned value, and is brought up to date only when a query touches it.
:::

::: {.p-approach}
1. **Eager.** Type 1 writes all $N$ cells. $O(N)$ per assignment, $O(NQ)$ total, too slow at $2\cdot10^5 \times 2\cdot10^5$. This function is the model for the stress test.
2. **Reset only the touched cells.** After an assignment, only the cells changed by later type 2 queries differ from the base value. Keep `base` and a list of touched indices; an assignment resets only those cells (to "no offset") and sets `base`. Each touched index is reset once per time it was touched, so the total is $O(N + Q)$.
3. **Timestamps.** Keep `base`, a clock `epoch` (number of assignments so far) and `stamp[i]`. Before reading or adding at $i$, *refresh* it: if `stamp[i] != epoch`, set `val[i] = base`, `stamp[i] = epoch`. An assignment is `base = x; epoch += 1`. Every query is $O(1)$ worst case.

**Operation contract (final form).** Invariant **I**: the true value of $A_i$ is `val[i]` if `stamp[i] == epoch`, and `base` otherwise.

| Query | What it does | How **I** survives | Cost |
|---|---|---|---|
| `1 x` | `base = x`; `epoch += 1` | every stamp is now older than `epoch`, so every cell reads `base = x`, which is the truth | $O(1)$ |
| `2 i x` | refresh $i$, then `val[i] += x` | after the refresh `stamp[i] == epoch`, so `val[i]` is the true value before and after the add | $O(1)$ |
| `3 i` | refresh $i$, print `val[i]` | refresh does not change the true value | $O(1)$ |
:::

The buttons are in order. Each one keeps the previous solution and changes only what that form changes.

::: {.panel-tabset .solution-forms}

