:::

::: {.p-complexity}
**Eager:** $O(N)$ per assignment, $O(NQ)$ worst case in total; $O(N)$ space. **Reset only the touched cells:** $O(N + Q)$ in total (each touched entry is reset once), but a single assignment can still cost $O(Q)$. **Timestamps:** $O(1)$ worst case per query, $O(N + Q)$ in total; $O(N)$ space for the stamps.
:::

::: {.p-edge}
- **Overflow**: values reach $10^9 + 2\cdot10^5 \cdot 10^9$, far beyond 32 bits; use 64-bit integers (`long long`).
- **The initial array** counts as written at epoch $0$, so `stamp` starts at $0$ and no refresh happens before the first assignment.
- **Several assignments in a row**: only the last `base` matters, and the clock comparison `stamp[i] != epoch` does not care how many epochs a cell missed.
:::

::: {.callout-warning title="Common mistakes"}
- **Refreshing after the add** instead of before: the add would be applied to a stale value and then overwritten by `base`.
- **Comparing `stamp[i] < epoch` with a clock that can wrap**: with a 32-bit clock and at most $2\cdot10^5$ assignments this cannot happen here, but in long-running systems it can; `!=` with a wide counter is the safe test.
:::

**Variants.** The same refresh-on-touch pattern implements "reset all to zero" in $O(1)$ for arrays reused across test cases (@sec-arr-multitest), and per-row versions of it give "assign a whole row" queries on a matrix (@sec-mat).

::: {.practice}
- [LC 1622 · Fancy Sequence](https://leetcode.com/problems/fancy-sequence/){difficulty="Hard"} — "add to all" and "multiply all" queries: keep one global affine map and store each element relative to the map in force when it was appended, the timestamp idea with a transform instead of a value.
:::


[**From a clock to a flag.** "Assign all" needs a clock, because each cell must know *whether* it missed an assignment. "Flip all" is its own inverse, so only the *parity* of missed flips matters: the clock collapses to one bit, and each stored bit is read through it.]{.flow-bridge}

### Problem: Design Bitset {#sec-ds-p5-bitset}

::: {.p-statement}
Implement a `Bitset(size)` of `size` bits, all $0$ at the start, with:

- `fix(idx)` / `unfix(idx)` — set bit `idx` to $1$ / to $0$;
- `flip()` — invert **every** bit;
- `all()` — are all bits $1$? `one()` — is at least one bit $1$? `count()` — how many bits are $1$?
- `toString()` — the bits as a string, character $i$ = bit $i$.

$1 \le$ `size` $\le 10^5$, at most $10^5$ calls in total, and at most $5$ calls to `toString`. The sample on `Bitset(5)`: `fix(3), fix(1), flip(), all() = false, unfix(0), flip(), one() = true, unfix(0), count() = 2, toString() = "01010"`.

Source: [LC 2166 · Design Bitset](https://leetcode.com/problems/design-bitset/){.solved difficulty="Medium"}
:::

::: {.p-intuition}
`flip()` touches every bit, so doing it eagerly costs $O(n)$ per call. Store the bits *relative to* a global flag instead: the true bit $i$ is `stored[i] XOR flipped`. A flip only toggles the flag and replaces the count of ones by `size - ones`. This is a lazy reset (@sec-ds-a5) whose "clock" has only two values.
:::

::: {.p-approach}
1. **Plain list.** A list of bits; `flip` inverts every entry and `count` sums the list. $O(n)$ for `flip`, `all`, `one`, `count`. This class is the naive model.
2. **Keep the count.** Maintain `ones` on every `fix`/`unfix`/`flip`, so `all`, `one` and `count` are $O(1)$. `flip` is still $O(n)$.
3. **Lazy flip flag.** Keep `flipped` and store each bit XOR the flag. `fix(i)` makes the true bit $1$, which means `stored[i] = 1 ^ flipped`; `flip()` toggles `flipped` and sets `ones = size - ones`. Every call except `toString` is $O(1)$.

**Operation contract (final form).** Invariant **I**: the true bit $i$ equals `stored[i] ^ flipped`, and `ones` is the number of true $1$ bits.

| Operation | What it does | How **I** survives | Cost |
|---|---|---|---|
| `fix(i)` / `unfix(i)` | if the true bit differs from the target, set `stored[i] = target ^ flipped` and adjust `ones` by $\pm1$ | one stored bit and the count change together | $O(1)$ |
| `flip()` | `flipped ^= 1`; `ones = size - ones` | every true bit inverts, so ones and zeros swap counts | $O(1)$ |
| `all()`, `one()`, `count()` | `ones == size`, `ones > 0`, `ones` | read only | $O(1)$ |
| `toString()` | build `stored[i] ^ flipped` for all $i$ | read only | $O(n)$, at most 5 calls |
:::

The buttons are in order. Each one keeps the previous solution and changes only what that form changes.

::: {.panel-tabset .solution-forms}

#### Plain list

