```cpp
vector<int> recentFormula(int n, const vector<int>& p) {
    vector<int> firsts; unordered_set<int> seen;
    for (int t = 0; t < (int)p.size(); ++t)
        if (!seen.count(p[t])) { seen.insert(p[t]); firsts.push_back(t + 1); }
    vector<int> ans(n, -1);
    for (int i = 1; i <= n; ++i) {
        int k = n - i;
        if (k < (int)firsts.size()) ans[i - 1] = firsts[k];
    }
    return ans;
}
```
:::

:::

::: {.p-complexity}
**Move-to-front simulation:** $O(n)$ per action (the membership search and the shift), $O(nm)$ in total; $O(n)$ space for the field. **First-arrival clock:** $O(1)$ expected per action (one hash-set lookup), $O(n + m)$ in total; $O(m)$ space for `seen`. **Arrival-rank formula:** $O(n + m)$ in total, one pass over the actions and one over the posts; $O(m)$ space for `seen` and `firsts`, plus $O(n)$ for the answer.
:::

::: {.p-edge}
- **Fewer distinct arrivals than posts**: if $|firsts| < n$, the surviving initial posts (indices $1 \ldots n-|firsts|$) answer $-1$; the `k < len(firsts)` guard handles it without a special case.
- **A repeat of an already-shown post** reorders the top but evicts nobody — counting it as an arrival would wrongly kill an extra initial post.
- **The drain direction**: post $n$ (bottom) leaves on the first distinct arrival, post $1$ on the $n$-th — the index $k = n - i$ encodes "from the back," matching that the field pushes out its tail.
:::

::: {.callout-warning title="Common mistakes"}
- **Simulating the array order** to answer and paying $O(n)$ per action, when only the *count of distinct arrivals* and their *times* matter. The field's internal order is irrelevant to the initial posts' fates.
- **Off-by-one on the arrival rank**: post $i$ maps to `firsts[n-i]`, not `firsts[i]`; the bottom post dies first.
- **Worrying about a new post that is pushed out and comes back.** A new post can only reach the bottom after all initial posts are gone, and from then on the initial posts' answers are fixed; so `seen` never needs to forget anything.
:::

**Variants.** When arriving ids may also be initial posts ($p_t \le n$), an initial post can be moved back to the top and the drain order is no longer fixed; then the field must really be simulated, with a linked list plus a `post -> node` map for $O(1)$ moves.

::: {.practice}
- [CF 500C · New Year Book Reading](https://codeforces.com/problemset/problem/500/C){rating="1600"} — a move-to-front stack of books where the cost of each read is the weight above the book; simulate it, then notice that the first-read order fixes the initial stack.
:::


[**From timestamps to running totals.** Recent Actions answers from recorded times. Underground System must answer averages over a growing set of trips; it folds each finished trip into a per-route `(sum, count)` the moment it ends and pops the traveller's open record, so nothing is ever re-summed or swept.]{.flow-bridge}

### Problem: Design Underground System {#sec-ds-p5-underground}

::: {.p-statement}
Model a metro system with three calls:

- `checkIn(id, stationName, t)` — traveller `id` starts at `stationName` at time `t`;
- `checkOut(id, stationName, t)` — the same traveller ends at `stationName` at time `t`;
- `getAverageTime(startStation, endStation)` — the average duration of **all trips so far** that went from `startStation` to `endStation`.

Calls are consistent: a traveller checks out only after checking in, is in at most one trip at a time, and `getAverageTime` is only asked about a route with at least one finished trip. $1 \le$ `id`, `t` $\le 10^6$, station names have at most $10$ characters, there are at most $2\cdot10^4$ calls, and answers within $10^{-5}$ are accepted. The first LeetCode sample answers its four queries with `14.0`, `11.0`, `11.0` and `12.0`.

Source: [LC 1396 · Design Underground System](https://leetcode.com/problems/design-underground-system/){.solved difficulty="Medium"}
:::

::: {.p-intuition}
An average needs only two numbers per route: the sum of the durations and their count. So fold each finished trip into `route -> (sum, count)` at `checkOut`, and `getAverageTime` becomes one division. The trip in progress lives in a small map `id -> (station, t_in)` that `checkIn` writes and `checkOut` pops: the record is cleared the moment it has been used, so no later sweep is needed.
:::

::: {.p-approach}
1. **Store every trip.** `route -> list of durations`; `getAverageTime` sums and divides the whole list. $O(\text{trips on the route})$ per query. This class is the model for the stress tests.
2. **Running totals.** `route -> (sum, count)`; `checkOut` folds the new duration in, `getAverageTime` is $O(1)$ (@fig-ds-p5-underground).
3. **One mutable record per route.** The same fold, with `total[route]` a mutable `[sum, n]` updated in place (a `pair` in C++, keyed by one string `start + ">" + end`), so a check-out does one lookup instead of a read and a write.

**Operation contract (final form).** Invariant **I**: `open` holds exactly the travellers currently in a trip, with their start; for every route, `total[route]` is the (sum, count) of its finished trips.

| Operation | What it does | How **I** survives | Cost |
|---|---|---|---|
| `checkIn(id, s, t)` | `open[id] = (s, t)` | the traveller is now in a trip | $O(1)$ expected |
| `checkOut(id, e, t)` | `(s, t0) = open.pop(id)`; add `t - t0` and $1$ to `total[(s, e)]` | the trip leaves `open` and joins its route's fold | $O(1)$ expected |
| `getAverageTime(s, e)` | `sum / count` of `total[(s, e)]` | read only | $O(1)$ expected |
:::

